Description: Use the ELF basename for unnamed single-executable modules
Author: Peios maintainers <packaging@peios.org>
Origin: vendor
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-09-16

The standard -e argument reports an empty module name. With -m -d, unstrip
therefore opens the output directory as a file, breaking make-debug-archive
for a single executable. Resolve the actual ELF path and use its basename
only for this empty-name case. Named modules retain their existing names.
The mandatory staged gate builds an archive from a debug executable via -e.

--- a/src/unstrip.c
+++ b/src/unstrip.c
@@ -2455,8 +2455,25 @@
   const char *name = dwfl_module_info (mod, NULL, NULL, NULL,
 				       NULL, NULL, &file, NULL);
 
+  if (modnames && name[0] == '\0')
+    {
+      /* A single -e input has an empty module name.  Resolve its ELF path
+         and use the basename instead of trying to write OUTPUT-DIRECTORY.  */
+      GElf_Addr bias;
+      (void) dwfl_module_getelf (mod, &bias);
+      (void) dwfl_module_info (mod, NULL, NULL, NULL,
+                              NULL, NULL, &file, NULL);
+      if (file != NULL)
+        {
+          const char *slash = strrchr (file, '/');
+          name = slash == NULL ? file : slash + 1;
+        }
+    }
+
   if (file == NULL && ignore)
     return;
+  if ((modnames && name[0] == '\0') || (!modnames && file == NULL))
+    error_exit (0, _("cannot determine output file name"));
 
   char *output_file = xasprintf ("%s/%s", output_dir, modnames ? name : file);
 
